---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj29.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Five-Step Model" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Over-Time vs Point-in-Time Recognition" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Replacing Fragmented Prior Guidance" },
        { id: "s6", label: "A NOTABLE CONTRAST — J-GAAP Kept Pace With the Current International Standard Here" },
        { id: "s7", label: "A Practical Expedient — the Short-Period Domestic Sales Alternative" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Allocating a Transaction Price Across Performance Obligations" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Over-Time Recognition for a Long-Term Contract" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A landmark, thoroughly converged standard</div>
        <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 29 introduced a COMPREHENSIVE, PRINCIPLES-BASED revenue recognition framework — built AROUND a FIVE-STEP model determining WHEN and HOW MUCH revenue to recognise for contracts with customers. It REPLACED Japan's PREVIOUS, more FRAGMENTED revenue guidance (including the percentage-of-completion framework under ASBJ Statement No. 15 — see the companion poster). Genuinely NOTABLY, this Statement represents a case where J-GAAP DIRECTLY ADOPTED the CURRENT jointly-developed international standard (IFRS 15/ASC 606), rather than LAGGING behind an OLDER, superseded model (Section 6).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Revenue from contracts with customers</div>
        <div class="principle">This Statement applies to REVENUE arising from CONTRACTS with customers for the sale of GOODS or PROVISION of services in the ORDINARY course of business — EXCLUDING certain scoped-out items such as lease revenue, insurance contracts, and financial instruments (governed by their OWN respective standards).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Five-Step Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Directly mirroring IFRS 15/ASC 606's structure</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-04-26">
            <span>Step</span>
            <span>Description</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-04-26"><span>1</span><span>Identify the CONTRACT with the customer</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-04-26"><span>2</span><span>Identify the PERFORMANCE OBLIGATIONS (distinct promises) within the contract</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-04-26"><span>3</span><span>Determine the TRANSACTION PRICE</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-04-26"><span>4</span><span>ALLOCATE the transaction price to each performance obligation, based on RELATIVE standalone selling price</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-04-26"><span>5</span><span>Recognise revenue AS (or WHEN) each performance obligation is SATISFIED</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Over-Time vs Point-in-Time Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The successor framework to the old percentage-of-completion analysis</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">A performance obligation is satisfied OVER TIME if the customer SIMULTANEOUSLY receives and consumes benefits AS the entity performs, the entity's performance CREATES or ENHANCES an asset the customer CONTROLS as it's created, or the asset has NO ALTERNATIVE use and the entity has an ENFORCEABLE right to payment for performance completed to date — OTHERWISE, revenue is recognised at a SINGLE POINT IN TIME, when control transfers. This GENERAL framework now GOVERNS what was PREVIOUSLY analysed under ASBJ Statement No. 15's separate percentage-of-completion/completed-contract terminology (see the companion poster).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Replacing Fragmented Prior Guidance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">A single, unified model replacing scattered topic-specific rules</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">BEFORE this Statement, Japanese revenue recognition guidance was SCATTERED across VARIOUS topic-specific sources — the SEPARATE construction contracts standard (ASBJ Statement No. 15), narrower software revenue practices, and general accounting PRINCIPLES without a single, COMPREHENSIVE framework. ASBJ Statement No. 29 CONSOLIDATED all of this into ONE unified, PRINCIPLES-BASED model — a GENUINE improvement in CONSISTENCY across industries and transaction types.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>A NOTABLE CONTRAST — J-GAAP Kept Pace With the Current International Standard Here</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Unlike financial instruments, leases, and insurance, this is a case of direct, current-standard adoption</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">ELSEWHERE in this poster series, a RECURRING pattern has emerged: J-GAAP standards (Financial Instruments under ASBJ Statement No. 10, the OLD Lease Transactions standard No. 13) REMAINED anchored to OLDER, superseded international models (IAS 39, IAS 17) for EXTENDED periods AFTER IFRS itself had MOVED ON to newer standards (IFRS 9, IFRS 16). Revenue Recognition is GENUINELY DIFFERENT — ASBJ Statement No. 29 was developed to DIRECTLY MIRROR the CURRENT, jointly-developed international standard (IFRS 15/ASC 606) from the OUTSET, WITHOUT the SAME multi-year LAG seen elsewhere. This makes it a USEFUL, CONTRASTING example illustrating that J-GAAP's convergence PACE varies MEANINGFULLY by TOPIC AREA, rather than following a single, uniform pattern across the entire suite.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>A Practical Expedient — the Short-Period Domestic Sales Alternative</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">A minor J-GAAP-specific simplification</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">J-GAAP permits a MINOR practical EXPEDIENT for DOMESTIC sales where the period between SHIPMENT and the customer's ACCEPTANCE (control transfer) is SHORT — allowing revenue recognition UPON SHIPMENT (or delivery) as a REASONABLE approximation, WITHOUT requiring a SEPARATE, precise control-transfer analysis for EVERY individual short-cycle domestic transaction — a genuinely MINOR, PRACTICAL simplification not necessarily present in the SAME explicit form under IFRS 15 itself.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Allocating a Transaction Price Across Performance Obligations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Splitting a bundled contract by relative standalone selling price</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A bundled hardware-and-service contract</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A company sells a bundle of hardware (standalone selling price ¥800,000) and a 1-year maintenance service (standalone selling price ¥200,000) for a combined ¥900,000.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">The ¥900,000 transaction price is ALLOCATED based on RELATIVE standalone selling price: hardware = ¥900,000 × (¥800,000 ÷ ¥1,000,000) = <strong>¥720,000</strong> (recognised at the POINT IN TIME the hardware is delivered); maintenance service = ¥900,000 × (¥200,000 ÷ ¥1,000,000) = <strong>¥180,000</strong> (recognised OVER TIME across the 1-year service period).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Over-Time Recognition for a Long-Term Contract</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The successor to the old percentage-of-completion analysis</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: A customised, no-alternative-use construction project</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A construction company builds a CUSTOM facility with NO alternative use to the builder, with an ENFORCEABLE right to payment for work completed to date, under a ¥600,000,000 contract. By year end, 45% of the work is complete.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Since the criteria for OVER-TIME recognition are met, the company recognises revenue of ¥600,000,000 × 45% = <strong>¥270,000,000</strong> for the year — the SAME general economic OUTCOME as the OLD percentage-of-completion method under ASBJ Statement No. 15, now analysed WITHIN this Statement's general five-step framework.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 29 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Disaggregation of revenue by category</span>
          <span>Information about performance obligations, including timing of satisfaction and significant payment terms</span>
          <span>Significant judgements made in applying the five-step model</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Identifying distinct performance obligations within a bundled contract</span>
              <span>Whether a performance obligation is satisfied over time or at a point in time</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Standalone selling prices used for transaction price allocation</span>
              <span>Variable consideration estimates</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IFRS 15 / ASC 606</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-1-1">
            <span>Feature</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 29)</span>
            <span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 15 / ASC 606</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Five-step model</span><span>YES</span><span>YES — converged, jointly developed</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-1-1"><span>Short-period domestic sales expedient</span><span>Available as a minor practical simplification</span><span>No explicit equivalent, though similar outcomes may arise in practice</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is one of the MOST genuinely converged, CURRENT-standard-aligned areas in the ENTIRE J-GAAP suite — a useful REMINDER that J-GAAP's convergence STATUS varies substantially by TOPIC, rather than being uniformly "behind" or "aligned" across the board.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every revenue recognition event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14">
            <span>Event</span>
            <span>Statement(s) affected</span>
            <span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Revenue recognition (over time or point in time)</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> <span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Based on satisfaction of performance obligations</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2">
            <span>Standard</span>
            <span>Connection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 15</span><span>Construction Contracts — its substance is now generally absorbed into this Statement's general over-time recognition criteria</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 30</span><span>Fair Value Measurement — another example of a standard genuinely aligned with the current international framework</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Performance obligation</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A distinct promise within a contract to transfer a good or service to a customer, the unit of account for revenue recognition.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last">
          <div class="glossary-term">Standalone selling price</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The price at which a good or service would be sold separately, used to allocate a bundled transaction price across performance obligations.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)
        <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)
        <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部
        <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)
        <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
